<Layout
  title="Intel GPU D270 - Hardware - Rycode"
  description="Intel GPU D270 is an inference data center GPU on Xe3P: Intel states 1.3 PFLOPS FP8, 160 GB LPDDR5X on Intel's card, up to 480 GB on partner cards, 1.5 TB/s, 350 W air-cooled PCIe."
  variant="ai"
>
  <article class="ai-model">
    <p class="ai-model-kicker">Hardware · Chips</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">Intel GPU D270</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">The Intel GPU D270 is a data center accelerator built only for inference, which means running an already trained AI model to answer requests, not training one. It is the first card in Intel's GPU D-series, formerly code-named Crescent Island, on Intel's Xe3P architecture. Intel announced it on 12 October 2026 at the Open Compute Project (OCP) Global Summit, a yearly data center hardware conference <a href="#source-5">[5]</a>, and shared new technical details in its OCP newsroom post <a href="#source-1">[1]</a>. Intel first announced Crescent Island on 14 October 2025 <a href="#source-3">[3]</a>.</p>
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">The card</h2>
    <p>Intel states the figures below <a href="#source-1">[1]</a>, <a href="#source-2">[2]</a>. Intel does not say whether the FP8 compute figure is dense or sparse.</p>
    <div class="ai-model-table-wrap">
      <table class="ai-model-table ai-model-table--prose">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Figure</th>
            <th scope="col">Intel GPU D270</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Architecture</td><td>32 Xe3P cores, 256 XMX Xe3P matrix engines</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Compute</td><td>1.3 PFLOPS FP8</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Number formats</td><td>From native low-precision inference to FP64 scientific computing; Intel does not list formats</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Memory type</td><td>LPDDR5X</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Memory size (Intel branded PCIe card)</td><td>160 GB LPDDR5X</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Memory size (partner ODM cards)</td><td>Up to 480 GB LPDDR5X per card</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Memory bandwidth</td><td>1.5 TB/s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Power</td><td>350 W, air-cooled</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Form factor</td><td>Standard PCIe 5.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cooling</td><td>Passive cooling in existing racks; no liquid-cooling retrofit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Model size, one card</td><td>Up to 300 billion parameters on a single card</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Model size, eight cards</td><td>More than one trillion parameters in an eight-GPU system</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Status</td><td>Customer sampling underway</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>A PFLOPS is a thousand trillion math operations per second. FP8 stores each number in 8 bits. LPDDR5X is low-power memory like that in laptops and phones, not HBM (high-bandwidth memory stacked next to the chip). PCIe is the standard slot plug-in cards use in ordinary servers. Parameters are the numbers a model learned in training; more parameters need more memory. ODM means a contract maker who builds cards under its own brand. Air-cooled and passive here mean the server's fans move heat, with no water lines.</p>

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Intel card vs partner cards</h2>
    <p>Intel states that its own branded PCIe card carries 160 GB of LPDDR5X, while the design lets partners build ODM branded cards with flexible options up to 480 GB <a href="#source-1">[1]</a>. Reported by Moor Insights & Strategy: Dell, HPE, Lenovo, and Supermicro are named as system partners, and Moor says Intel's own card ships with 160 GB while ODM partners can build cards with up to 480 GB <a href="#source-4">[4]</a>. Intel does not list which partner ships a 480 GB card or when.</p>
    <p>Reported by Moor: the 300-billion-parameter single-card claim really belongs to the 480 GB partner cards; on the 160 GB Intel card, a model that size fits only at roughly 4-bit precision, with little room left for context <a href="#source-4">[4]</a>. Intel does not state what precision or context length the 300B claim assumes.</p>

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">What Intel claims</h2>
    <p>These are Intel's own claims, not outside test results. No independent tests yet.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Intel calls the D270 an inference-optimized data center GPU designed for the agentic AI era, and says it moved chip area from graphics and ray-tracing pipelines to Xe3P cores and matrix compute, so it is not a graphics card <a href="#source-1">[1]</a>, <a href="#source-2">[2]</a>.</li>
      <li>Intel targets enterprise AI, SaaS, GPUaaS, and government or sovereign-cloud deployments that need memory-rich, power-efficient AI acceleration <a href="#source-2">[2]</a>. Workloads include agentic AI, long-context RAG (retrieval-augmented generation, looking up documents before answering), assistants, coding assistants, document understanding, multimodal models, diffusion, and high-concurrency serving <a href="#source-2">[2]</a>.</li>
      <li>Intel's pitch against HBM chips: fewer GPUs and no HBM, working within existing data centers without specialized rack or cooling infrastructure <a href="#source-1">[1]</a>, <a href="#source-2">[2]</a>. For contrast, the <a href="/ai/hardware/chips/rubin/">Rubin</a> GPU is listed on this site at 19.2 TB/s of memory bandwidth on liquid-cooled racks <a href="#source-8">[8]</a>. The D270 trades speed for capacity: slower memory (1.5 TB/s) that can hold more per card, in ordinary air-cooled servers.</li>
      <li>Reported by Moor: capacity is the pitch, not speed; Moor compares the D270 with PCIe server cards such as Nvidia's RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition, not flagship HBM chips <a href="#source-4">[4]</a>.</li>
      <li>Intel's product page footnote says performance projections versus RTX Pro 6000 are based on publicly available information and Intel internal analysis as of February 2026; the page we cite shows no number for that comparison <a href="#source-2">[2]</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Software</h2>
    <p>Intel states the card runs on a common Xe software foundation that supports software portability across Intel GPU platforms <a href="#source-1">[1]</a>. The product page lists zero-day support for leading open models on vLLM and the open PyTorch ecosystem <a href="#source-2">[2]</a>. vLLM is popular open-source software for serving models, and PyTorch is the most common AI coding toolkit. Intel cites Day 0 support for Meta's Muse Glimmer <a href="#source-1">[1]</a>. Reported by Moor: SGLang, another model-serving program, is also in the stack <a href="#source-4">[4]</a>.</p>

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">What is not known</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Price. Intel names no price; the product page asks readers to connect with Intel for updates <a href="#source-2">[2]</a>.</li>
      <li>Intel-stated general availability date. Intel says only that sampling is underway <a href="#source-1">[1]</a>. Reported by Moor: broader availability in 2027; no Intel page we cite gives 2027 <a href="#source-4">[4]</a>.</li>
      <li>Whether the 1.3 PFLOPS FP8 figure is dense or sparse <a href="#source-1">[1]</a>.</li>
      <li>Which partners ship 480 GB cards, and when.</li>
      <li>What precision and context length the 300-billion-parameter-on-one-card claim assumes, and whether it holds on the 160 GB Intel card.</li>
      <li>Independent benchmark results. MLPerf Inference v6.1 (September 2026) shows Intel with Xeon and Arc Pro B70 systems, not D270 <a href="#source-6">[6]</a>. Intel's v6.0 entry (1 April 2026) used Arc Pro B70 and B65 <a href="#source-7">[7]</a>. Reported by Moor: there is no independent performance data yet <a href="#source-4">[4]</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Sources</h2>
    <ol class="ai-model-sources">
      {sources.map((source, index) => (
        <li id={`source-${index + 1}`}>
          <a href={source.href}>{source.label}</a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </article>
</Layout>
